window.__SLIDES&&window.__SLIDES('f2',{"s":["<section id=\"f2-tldr\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assistance games · 2016–2025</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Helping a human whose goal the robot cannot see</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px\">\n<div style=\"width:880px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Background · before the series</p><ul style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\"><li><b>IRL</b> · Inverse RL (Ng &amp; Russell; Abbeel &amp; Ng) infers a reward from behavior assumed optimal.</li><li><b>Optimal teaching</b> · Cakmak &amp; Lopes: a teacher picks examples that make learning efficient.</li><li><b>Hidden-goal MDPs</b> · Fern et al. cast the human as part of the environment, not a partner who adapts.</li><li><b>Pragmatic-pedagogic</b> · Fisac et al. solve a CIRL game with a teaching human; Contribution prefs and AssistanceZero both cite it.</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Problem</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">A robot or assistant should maximize a reward that only the human knows. It must infer that reward from the human&#x27;s actions while helping at the same time.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Idea</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Treat help as a game with one shared reward, hidden from the robot. Later work makes it practical: online Bayesian belief, then learned predictors plus tree search.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/f2_tldr.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of the key papers in this work tree, drawn by year with arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:736px;height:260px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">CIRL (2016) defines the game; AssistanceZero (2025) solves it at scale.</p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#EFEBE0;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Terms</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#1E8074\">Assistance game</b> · human and robot share a reward only the human knows</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#1E8074\">CIRL</b> · Cooperative Inverse RL: the first assistance game</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#1E8074\">Value alignment</b> · making the robot&#x27;s objective match the human&#x27;s</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#1E8074\">Self-model</b> · MIRROR: the robot&#x27;s own RL model, reused to model a human</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Assistance games and value alignment</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">5 papers · 2016–2025 · CIRL</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f2-assume\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assistance games · Assumptions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Assistance games assume a reward only the human knows</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#1E8074\">A hidden human reward</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">The human knows what is valued; the robot starts uncertain. Value alignment gives the user a hidden linear value.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#1E8074\">The human is cooperative</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">The human is noisily rational and helpful, not adversarial or deceptive. MIRROR instead assumes a human close to the robot.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#1E8074\">A fixed human model</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">The human behaves as a stationary policy (Contribution prefs, AssistanceZero), not one that adapts to the robot.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#1E8074\">Small, known worlds</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Small known tasks until AssistanceZero, whose Minecraft house has over 10^400 possible goals (prior work under 20).</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#1E8074\">Simulated or small human data</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Evidence uses simulated humans and small studies: n = 5 to 135 (CIRL has none).</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:20px;align-items:center\"><div style=\"width:760px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7F5;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Builds on</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">Inverse RL (Ng &amp; Russell; Abbeel &amp; Ng), Dec-POMDP reduction, ITAL teacher-aware learning, AlphaZero, social projection theory.</p></div><svg width=\"48\" height=\"30\" viewBox=\"0 0 48 30\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,9.0 L28.799999999999997,9.0 L28.799999999999997,0 L48.0,15.0 L28.799999999999997,30.0 L28.799999999999997,21.0 L0,21.0 Z\" fill=\"#1E8074\"/></svg><div style=\"width:816px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7F5;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">So the series achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">CIRL&#x27;s POMDP reduction; online preference learning (Contribution prefs); a goal-predicting AlphaZero that helped 16 people (AssistanceZero).</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Assistance games and value alignment</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">5 papers · 2016–2025 · CIRL</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f2-method\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assistance games · Evolution</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">From a theory game to learned goal and human models</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"background:#E3F7F5;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.35\">Each step makes the hidden reward easier to infer online: formal game, then belief or model of the human, then learned predictors in a planner.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/f2_method.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of every paper in the work tree by year, arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:1664px;height:470px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Each arrow points from a paper to the paper that builds on it. Dark pills mark the key papers. Papers without an arrow are tied to the series only by baseline comparisons.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Assistance games and value alignment</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">5 papers · 2016–2025 · CIRL</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f2-stages\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assistance games · Stages</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">The series unfolds in three stages</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:16px;align-items:stretch\"><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #C8651B;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#C8651B\">1 · Formalise the game (2016)</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2016</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7F5;border:2px solid #1E8074;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">CIRL</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Two-player game with a shared hidden reward, reduced to a POMDP; the human best-responds by teaching.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#1E8074\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #3E6FD9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#3E6FD9\">2 · Model the human (2022)</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2022</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7F5;border:2px solid #1E8074;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">MIRROR</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7F5;border:2px solid #1E8074;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Value alignment</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Not stated as games: MIRROR fits small implants to a self-model; Value alignment infers values from feedback.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#1E8074\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #8E4FC9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#8E4FC9\">3 · Infer and plan (2023-25)</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2023–2025</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7F5;border:2px solid #1E8074;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Contribution prefs</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3F7F5;border:2px solid #1E8074;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">AssistanceZero</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Back to CIRL&#x27;s game: Bayesian IRL belief with info-seeking; AlphaZero with goal and human-action heads.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Assistance games and value alignment</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">5 papers · 2016–2025 · CIRL</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f2-s01\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assistance games · CIRL 2016 · 1 of 5</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">A shared hidden reward turns demos into teaching</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on IRL (Ng &amp; Russell; Abbeel &amp; Ng; Ziebart et al.) and the Dec-POMDP reduction (Nayyar et al.). No earlier member of this tree.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Both get the human&#x27;s reward; only the human knows it</li><li>Players coordinate on a joint policy beforehand</li><li>Linear rewards; robot runs MaxEnt IRL</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">The CIRL game, an exact POMDP reduction (belief over the reward suffices) and a best-response demonstration that teaches the robot.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f2_s01.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3F7F5;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Contribution prefs and AssistanceZero reuse the game; MIRROR and Value alignment do not cite it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7F5;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">2D grid navigation, 500 reward samples: teaching beats expert demos on all 3 measures (F &gt; 962).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Cooperative Inverse Reinforcement Learning</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Hadfield-Menell et al. · NeurIPS 2016</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f2-s02\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assistance games · MIRROR 2022 · 2 of 5</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Model the human as a small tweak of the robot</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on social projection theory, SLAC self-models and Assistive State Estimation (outside the tree). Cites no member; joins this line by topic.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Human is close to the robot&#x27;s own model</li><li>Robot can edit what the human sees</li><li>Implant types are chosen by hand</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Fit small perceptual and policy implants to a frozen RL self-model from few demos, then plan messages by imagining futures.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f2_s02.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7F5;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Gridworlds plus CARLA fog study: good models from 4 demos; far fewer collisions (n = 21).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">MIRROR: Differentiable Deep Social Projection for Assistive Human-Robot Communication</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Chen et al. · RSS 2022</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f2-s03\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assistance games · Value alignment 2022 · 3 of 5</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Robots infer values from feedback and explain</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on ITAL teacher-aware learning and a Boltzmann user model; contrasts IRL and XAI. Cites no member; Contribution prefs cites it as background.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Hidden linear value over four goals</li><li>Only accept/reject feedback from the user</li><li>User is noisy-rational and teaches</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Two levels of Theory of Mind (literal and pragmatic) to read feedback, plus explanations tuned to what the user finds satisfying.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f2_s03.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3F7F5;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Only a background citation in Contribution prefs; no later member reuses its method.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7F5;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Scout Exploration Game, 135 people: rank correlation tau 0.5 (full explanations) vs 0.2 (proposal only).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">In situ bidirectional human-robot value alignment</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Yuan et al. · Science Robotics 2022</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f2-s04\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assistance games · Contribution prefs 2023 · 4 of 5</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Learn a human&#x27;s job preferences, steering to learn</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on CIRL: its game, with a composite team, robot and human reward. Compares against Prag-Ped (a CIRL solution) and MaxEnt IRL. Cites Value alignment as background only.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Fixed hidden human reward over a known hypothesis set</li><li>Noisily rational, stationary human</li><li>Preferences cover only the human&#x27;s own actions</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">ICPL game and two online learners: BaL (Bayesian IRL belief) and BaISL (adds an information-gain bonus for informative human choices).</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f2_s04.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3F7F5;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Same Bayesian hidden-reward idea as AssistanceZero, but no later member builds on it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7F5;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">5-person pilot, last round: BaISL 86% of optimal reward vs Prag-Ped 48%.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Learning Human Contribution Preferences in Collaborative Human-Robot Tasks</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Zhao et al. · CoRL 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f2-s05\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assistance games · AssistanceZero 2025 · 5 of 5</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">AlphaZero with goal and human heads solves the game</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on CIRL&#x27;s assistance-game formulation (kind 0, hand-written edge kept), plus AlphaZero and piKL (outside). Cites no other member.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Hidden goal, one shared reward</li><li>Fixed human model (piKL, from 18 recorded episodes)</li><li>Structured goals, simulated 3D grid</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A recurrent AlphaZero with heads that predict the hidden goal and the human&#x27;s next action, so tree search can plan under goal uncertainty.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/f2_s05.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3F7F5;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Minecraft building game (MBAG): human actions 245 to 179 in simulation; helpfulness 3.1 vs 1.7 (16 people).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">AssistanceZero: Scalably Solving Assistance Games</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Laidlaw et al. · ICML 2025</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f2-eval\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assistance games · Evaluation</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Five different tests, so no shared scoreboard</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:560px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:20px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:22px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Benchmarks and baselines</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Grid navigation, gridworlds, a Minecraft building game and a robot-arm declutter task.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Humans are simulated in most tests; real users are 5 to 135 people.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Metrics differ: error, collisions, rank correlation, % of optimal, actions saved.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#E3F7F5;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Reading</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.35\">Numbers come from different tasks and metrics: read each against its own baseline, not across rows.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:20px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472;padding-bottom:4px\">Headline result of each paper</p><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#1E8074\">CIRL</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">2D grid, 500 reward samples</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Teaching demos beat expert demos on all 3 measures (F &gt; 962)</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#1E8074\">MIRROR</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">3 gridworlds + CARLA fog (n = 21)</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Good models from 4 demos; far fewer collisions than NC and BC</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#1E8074\">Value alignment</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Scout Exploration Game, n = 135</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Rank correlation tau 0.5 (full) vs 0.2 (proposal only)</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#1E8074\">Contribution prefs</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Declutter sim + pilot, n = 5</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Last round: BaISL 86% of optimal vs Prag-Ped 48%</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#1E8074\">AssistanceZero</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">MBAG Minecraft, human study n = 16</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Human actions 245 to 179; helpfulness 3.1 vs 1.7 (SFT)</p></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Assistance games and value alignment</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">5 papers · 2016–2025 · CIRL</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"f2-open\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#1E8074\">Assistance games · Open questions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">What the series still leaves open</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #1E8074;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#1E8074;width:56px\">1</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Human models are fixed or simulated; real humans adapt to the robot (AssistanceZero, Contribution prefs).</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #1E8074;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#1E8074;width:56px\">2</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Coordination is assumed: CIRL leaves how the pair agree on a joint policy to future work.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #1E8074;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#1E8074;width:56px\">3</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Tests are small: Contribution prefs has 5 people and no statistics; AssistanceZero&#x27;s human model uses 18 episodes.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Assistance games and value alignment</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">5 papers · 2016–2025 · CIRL</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>"],"n":["Members (year): CIRL (2016), MIRROR (2022), Value alignment (2022), Contribution prefs (2023), AssistanceZero (2025). Solid edges (2): AssistanceZero -&gt; CIRL (kind 0, hand-written note kept: solves the assistance-game formulation at scale) and Contribution prefs -&gt; CIRL (kind 0, my classification from reading the paper: its ICPL game is a CIRL-style game with a composite reward). One comparison/background edge: Contribution prefs cites Value alignment (kind 2; one sentence in the Introduction, not used further). MIRROR and Value alignment have no solid edge: neither cites CIRL, and no member cites MIRROR. They join by topic: assistants inferring hidden human mental state or values, with communication. So the line is honest but thin: a 2016 formalism with two descendants, plus two 2022 papers solving a neighbouring problem with different tools (social projection and Theory of Mind). Background (from the papers&#x27; own related work): IRL (Ng &amp; Russell; Abbeel &amp; Ng), optimal teaching (Cakmak &amp; Lopes), hidden-goal MDPs (Fern et al.), pragmatic-pedagogic alignment (Fisac et al., cited by Contribution prefs and AssistanceZero) and RLHF (Bai et al., cited by AssistanceZero). Survey vocabulary: all five sit mainly in Mirsky et al. ST2 (modelling the human) and ST3 (action selection). MIRROR and Value alignment add communication; CIRL is also ST1 (formalism). Albrecht &amp; Stone family: goal/preference inference; MIRROR and Value alignment also recursive reasoning. The assistance-game line is largely outside both surveys&#x27; main families.","Shared assumptions: (1) a reward only the human knows (CIRL Def. 1; Value alignment; Contribution prefs; AssistanceZero); MIRROR instead assumes a human differing from the robot&#x27;s self-model by small implants. (2) A cooperative, noisily rational human (Boltzmann) in Value alignment and Contribution prefs; AssistanceZero uses a fixed piKL human model. (3) A fixed human policy: Contribution prefs assumes a stationary expert policy; AssistanceZero notes a history-based model predicts real humans better than its Markov formalism assumes. (4) Small known worlds, except AssistanceZero&#x27;s Minecraft house with over 10^400 goals (paper&#x27;s figure; prior work under 20 goals). (5) Evidence from simulated humans and small studies: n = 21 (MIRROR), 135 (Value alignment), 5 (Contribution prefs), 16 (AssistanceZero); CIRL has no human study. Relaxations: CIRL assumes perfect coordination on a joint policy, left to future work; Contribution prefs adds the robot&#x27;s own constraints as a second reward; AssistanceZero drops tiny goal sets. Edges used: the two kind-0 edges above; the rest are comparisons or none.","Stage 1, CIRL (2016): two-player Markov game with identical payoffs, reduced to a POMDP with |S|*|Theta| hidden states; belief over the reward is a sufficient statistic; best-response demonstrations teach. Stage 2 (2022), not game-based and not linked by citation: MIRROR learns implants on a frozen RL self-model and plans messages (cross-entropy method); Value alignment updates a value estimate from accept/reject feedback with two Theory-of-Mind levels and picks explanations with a hidden Markov model. Stage 3: Contribution prefs (2023) keeps CIRL&#x27;s setting and adds Bayesian IRL belief with a myopic information-gain bonus (BaISL); AssistanceZero (2025) fixes a human model so the game is a POMDP, then extends AlphaZero with goal and human-action prediction heads. Inheritance edges: CIRL -&gt; Contribution prefs and CIRL -&gt; AssistanceZero. Contribution prefs -&gt; Value alignment is only background. The 2022 papers are placed by topic.","Stage 1, CIRL (2016): two-player Markov game with identical payoffs, reduced to a POMDP with |S|*|Theta| hidden states; belief over the reward is a sufficient statistic; best-response demonstrations teach. Stage 2 (2022), not game-based and not linked by citation: MIRROR learns implants on a frozen RL self-model and plans messages (cross-entropy method); Value alignment updates a value estimate from accept/reject feedback with two Theory-of-Mind levels and picks explanations with a hidden Markov model. Stage 3: Contribution prefs (2023) keeps CIRL&#x27;s setting and adds Bayesian IRL belief with a myopic information-gain bonus (BaISL); AssistanceZero (2025) fixes a human model so the game is a POMDP, then extends AlphaZero with goal and human-action prediction heads. Inheritance edges: CIRL -&gt; Contribution prefs and CIRL -&gt; AssistanceZero. Contribution prefs -&gt; Value alignment is only background. The 2022 papers are placed by topic.","Hadfield-Menell, Dragan, Abbeel, Russell, NeurIPS 2016 (p57). PDF read is arXiv v4 (2024) with appendix. Edges: parent (kind 0) of AssistanceZero (hand-written, kept: it solves the assistance-game formulation) and of Contribution prefs (kind 0, my classification: ICPL is a CIRL-style two-player game with a composite reward). It has no parent inside this tree. Mechanism (Sec. 3): Def. 1 is a two-player Markov game with identical payoffs where only H observes theta. Theorem 1: any CIRL game reduces to a coordination-POMDP with |S|*|Theta| hidden states; Corollary 1: R&#x27;s belief over theta is a sufficient statistic. Theorem 3: demonstration-by-expert is not H&#x27;s best response to R&#x27;s best response (shown on a paperclip/staple example). Sec. 3.4: approximate best response picks trajectory tau maximizing phi(tau)^T theta - eta*||phi_theta - phi(tau)||^2; eta by cross-validation. Eval (Sec. 4, Fig. 2): 2 factors (expert vs best-response demos; 3 vs 10 features), N = 500 random reward parameters, R uses MaxEnt IRL. Measures: regret, KL, l2 error. br beats expert on every measure, all F &gt; 962, p &lt; .0001; the gap is smaller with 10 features. The paper has no table: bar values read by eye are only approximate (3 features, l2 error ~0.8 vs ~9.7; 10 features ~13.3 vs ~15.3). Caveats: perfect coordination is assumed and left to future work; the paper warns the reduced POMDP can still be very hard; no human-subject data.","Chen, Fong, Soh, RSS 2022 (p80). No edge to or from other members (it does not cite CIRL or Value alignment; no later member cites it). It sits in this tree by topic: an assistant that infers hidden human mental state to help, via communication rather than a shared-reward game. Mechanism (Sec. III): self-model is a multi-modal latent state-space model trained with SAC (CARLA) or DQN (gridworlds). Implants: perceptual (distance threshold filter) and policy (state-dependent residual); only implant parameters are fit by negative log-likelihood of human actions. Messages are chosen with the cross-entropy method to maximize task reward minus communication cost. Eval: 3 gridworlds (Driving, Search-and-Rescue, Bomb Defusal) vs BC and SQIL; good models from 20% of the data (4 trajectories). Human study: 21 drivers, within-subjects, 24 rounds each, CARLA dense fog. Collisions: MIRROR vs BC and NC significantly fewer, F(3,60) = 47.977, p &lt; .001. Collision means are only plotted (read by eye ~NC 2.9, BC 2.5, MIRROR-KL 0.5, MIRROR 0.6, approximate). 14 of 21 most comfortable with MIRROR (the other 7 picked MIRROR-KL, derived by subtraction); 18 of 21 chose it for drives over 1 hour. MIRROR-KL talked over 5x more without a significant benefit. BC had the best NLL but did not plan better messages. Caveats: depends on human-robot similarity; identifiability of implants is open; the appendix was not in the PDF.","Yuan et al., Science Robotics 2022 (p81). Edges: no parent in the tree; cited by Contribution prefs only as background on humans adapting to robot constraints (kind 2, checked in the text: cited as [23, 24] in one sentence of the Introduction). It does not cite CIRL. Mechanism: level 1 reads feedback literally (Boltzmann acceptance); level 2 simulates the level-1 update for all 8 feedback combinations per round and credits the one closest to the true value (closed form). Proposals use the current best plan, not the most informative query, to avoid hurting the user&#x27;s impression. Explanations are templates; a hidden Markov model over the user&#x27;s Likert satisfaction picks the style. Eval: 1 commander, 3 scouts, 20x20 partly seen map; 135 participants, 45 per group; 7 value functions; same robot policy, only explanation differs (proposal only, brief, full). Kendall tau of robots&#x27; estimate vs true value: 0.2, 0.4, 0.5 as quoted in the text (the paper does not state the progress point; Fig. 4A means at 100% read ~0.16/0.43/0.47, approximate). Both explanation groups beat proposal only from 25% of the game (P &lt; .001); brief and full do not differ. Only full explanations help users read the robots&#x27; values in the second half. Trust: no significant group difference. Caveats: no IRL baseline and no level-1 vs level-2 ablation in the main text; supplementary algorithm not in the PDF.","Zhao, Simmons, Admoni, CoRL 2023 (p67). Edges: child of CIRL (kind 0, my classification: ICPL is defined as a two-player game where only the human knows its reward, extended to balance several objectives; the paper says CIRL focuses on convergence to the human&#x27;s values while ICPL balances multiple objectives); child of Value alignment (kind 2: one background citation). Prag-Ped (Fisac et al.) is a baseline and is outside the tree. Mechanism (Sec. 4): belief b over human individual rewards; likelihood from an optimistic noisily-rational human (Boltzmann, thresholded with lambda = 0.9). BaL plans with value iteration under the belief; BaISL adds alpha times a one-step (myopic) information gain. Eval: simulated decluttering, 3 object types, 6 rounds, 100 random reward settings, 3 simulated human models. With rational humans BaL and BaISL reach optimal reward for the optimistic and expected-reward humans; Prag-Ped wins only with the pedagogic human it assumes. BaISL is not significantly better than BaL in simulation. Pilot (5 people, no statistics, 3 rounds): final round BaISL 86%, BaL 72%, MaxEnt IRL 62%, Prag-Ped 48% (text of Sec. 6; large error bars). Appendix 10 (Team Space Fortress, 20 configs): BaISL beats BaL and MaxEnt IRL. Caveats: constraints assumed to be reward functions; fixed human policy; pilot is tiny.","Laidlaw et al., ICML 2025 (p69). Edge: child of CIRL (kind 0, kept from the earlier hand-written note). The paper names CIRL and Fern et al. as the origin of assistance games; it does not cite Contribution prefs, MIRROR or Value alignment. Mechanism (Sec. 4): with a fixed human model an assistance game is a single-agent POMDP. PPO with a recurrent policy failed to help (Table 1). AssistanceZero adds a reward-parameter (goal) head and a human-action head to AlphaZero&#x27;s policy and value heads; MCTS uses them to estimate rewards and next states; extra KL term stops the goal head overfitting. Eval: Table 3 (piKL human model, 1,000 test episodes): human alone 245 +/- 4 actions; pretraining 240; SFT 241; assistance game 179 +/- 11; assistant builds 26.0 +/- 3.3 percent of the goal. Table 1 (imitation human model): PPO baseline assistant goal 0.0; best PPO variant (plus auxiliary loss, my label) 7.2; AssistanceZero 27.0, human actions 158 (human alone 200); no-test-time-MCTS gives 27.3, so the gain is not extra compute. Human study: 16 people, Latin square, helpfulness on 5 points: AssistanceZero 3.1 +/- 0.4, SFT 1.7 +/- 0.3, human assistant 4.0 +/- 0.5 (90% CIs). Human-action reduction vs building alone is significant (p &lt; .05, one-sided) but shown only as a box plot. Caveats: gap to a human assistant remains; the human model is fixed and trained on only 18 episodes; no full RLHF baseline (the authors argue RLHF does not fit).","All numbers are as written in each paper&#x27;s notes and are not comparable. CIRL: 500 reward samples, 2D grid; br beats expert on all measures, F &gt; 962, p &lt; .0001 (bar values by eye only). MIRROR: 21 drivers, CARLA fog; good models from 4 demos (20% of data); 14 of 21 most comfortable with MIRROR; collision means only plotted. Value alignment: 135 participants, 45 per group; Kendall tau 0.2 / 0.4 / 0.5 (proposal / brief / full), quoted in the text, progress point not stated. Contribution prefs: pilot n = 5, final round BaISL 86, BaL 72, MaxEnt IRL 62, Prag-Ped 48 (% of optimal); BaISL vs BaL not significant in simulation. AssistanceZero: Table 3 human actions 245 vs 179; Table 1 assistant goal 27.0 vs 7.2 best PPO variant; human study n = 16, helpfulness 3.1 vs 1.7 SFT vs 4.0 human assistant. Uncertainties: see the uncertain list.","All numbers are as written in each paper&#x27;s notes and are not comparable. CIRL: 500 reward samples, 2D grid; br beats expert on all measures, F &gt; 962, p &lt; .0001 (bar values by eye only). MIRROR: 21 drivers, CARLA fog; good models from 4 demos (20% of data); 14 of 21 most comfortable with MIRROR; collision means only plotted. Value alignment: 135 participants, 45 per group; Kendall tau 0.2 / 0.4 / 0.5 (proposal / brief / full), quoted in the text, progress point not stated. Contribution prefs: pilot n = 5, final round BaISL 86, BaL 72, MaxEnt IRL 62, Prag-Ped 48 (% of optimal); BaISL vs BaL not significant in simulation. AssistanceZero: Table 3 human actions 245 vs 179; Table 1 assistant goal 27.0 vs 7.2 best PPO variant; human study n = 16, helpfulness 3.1 vs 1.7 SFT vs 4.0 human assistant. Uncertainties: see the uncertain list."]});
